{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Both $1$ and $x^2$ are *perfect squares,* since $1=(\\blueD{1})^2$ and $x^2=(\\greenD{x})^2$.\n\n$1-x^2 = (\\blueD{1})^2-(\\greenD{x})^2$\n\nSo we can use the *difference of squares* pattern to factor.\n\n$\\blueD{a}^2 - \\greenD{b}^2 =(\\blueD{a}+\\greenD{b})(\\blueD{a}-\\greenD{b})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In this case, $\\blueD{a}=\\blueD{1}$ and $\\greenD{b}=\\greenD{x}$:\n\n$(\\blueD{1})^2 - (\\greenD{x})^2 =(\\blueD{1}+\\greenD{x})(\\blueD{1}-\\greenD{x})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$1-x^2=(1+x)(1-x)$\n\n*Remember that you can always check your factorization by expanding it.*","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Factor as the product of two binomials.**\n\n$1-x^2=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":0,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"\\left(1+x\\right)\\left(1-x\\right)"},{"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":1,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"-\\left(x+1\\right)\\left(x-1\\right)"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"static":false,"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
